  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of artificial intelligence has led to the emergence of code agents—autonomous systems capable of understanding, generating, and executing code. While these agents offer transformative potential for software development, they also introduce significant security and trustworthiness concerns. This paper reviews the current state of code agent research, focusing on red-teaming methodologies designed to identify vulnerabilities and assess the trustworthiness of these systems. We examine automated red-teaming approaches, their effectiveness in uncovering security risks, and the development of complementary blue-teaming strategies. Our analysis reveals that while significant progress has been made in identifying and mitigating code agent vulnerabilities, there remains a critical need for standardized evaluation frameworks and comprehensive safety benchmarks. We further discuss the implications of our findings for the responsible deployment of code agents in production environments.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe landscape of software development has been fundamentally transformed by the advent of large language models (LLMs) capable of generating and executing code. Code agents—AI systems that can autonomously understand programming tasks, generate code solutions, debug existing code, and execute programs—represent a significant leap forward in AI-assisted software development. From automated code generation to intelligent debugging and even autonomous software engineering, code agents are reshaping how we build and maintain software systems [1].\n\nHowever, the increasing capabilities of code agents also introduce novel security and trustworthiness challenges. Unlike traditional code generation tools, code agents can execute code dynamically, interact with their environment, and make autonomous decisions. This expanded capability introduces multiple attack surfaces and potential failure modes that must be carefully studied and mitigated. Security researchers have begun to systematically explore these vulnerabilities through red-teaming—the process of actively attempting to exploit or \"break\" AI systems to identify weaknesses before malicious actors do [2].\n\nThis paper presents a comprehensive review of research advances in code agents and red-teaming. We begin by examining the current state of code agent technology and its applications. We then discuss the methodology and findings of recent red-teaming efforts, including automated approaches that can systematically probe code agents for vulnerabilities. We further explore the emerging field of blue-teaming—developing defensive strategies and mitigations based on insights gained from red-teaming exercises.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\nThe intersection of code agents, AI safety, and red-teaming has been the subject of growing academic and industrial interest. Several foundational works have laid the groundwork for understanding and addressing the trustworthiness challenges of AI systems.\n\n### 2.1 Trustworthiness Assessment of Language Models\n\nA critical foundation for understanding code agent safety lies in the broader literature on trustworthiness of large language models. The DecodingTrust framework [3] provides a comprehensive assessment of trustworthiness in GPT models, evaluating them across multiple dimensions including truthfulness, fairness, robustness, safety, and bias. This work demonstrates that while language models exhibit impressive capabilities, they also display significant vulnerabilities to generating harmful content, exhibiting biases, and producing factually incorrect information. The findings from DecodingTrust provide important insights for understanding the trustworthiness challenges that extend to code agents, as these systems inherit capabilities from their underlying language model foundations.\n\nThe framework established by DecodingTrust has informed subsequent research on AI safety, including specific evaluations of code-related trustworthiness. By systematically assessing model behavior across diverse categories of risks, this work has helped establish baseline metrics for evaluating the safety of increasingly capable AI systems.\n\n### 2.2 Architectural Foundations\n\nThe development of code agents builds upon decades of research in deep learning and neural network architectures. The success of modern AI systems owes much to architectural innovations such as deep residual learning, which enables training of substantially deeper networks than previously possible [4]. The residual learning framework introduced in [4] revolutionized deep neural network design, allowing models with 100+ layers to be trained effectively and achieving state-of-the-art performance on visual recognition tasks.\n\nWhile [4] focuses on image recognition, the architectural principles it established have had far-reaching implications across AI research, including in the development of language models and code agents. The transformer architecture, which builds upon attention mechanisms, represents a further evolution that has become the foundation for modern code generation systems. Understanding these architectural foundations helps explain both the capabilities and limitations of current code agents.\n\n### 2.3 Code Agent Safety and Red-teaming\n\nRecent work has directly addressed the security implications of code agents. Research such as RedCode [5] has introduced risk benchmarks for evaluating code execution and generation capabilities, while subsequent work on RedCodeAgent [6] has developed automatic red-teaming methodologies for systematically testing code agents against diverse attack vectors. These tools enable researchers to identify vulnerabilities such as code injection, unsafe execution, and prompt manipulation attacks.\n\nThe evolution from manual to automated red-teaming represents an important trend in AI safety research. Automated approaches can systematically explore the vast attack surface of code agents, identifying vulnerabilities that might be missed by human testers. However, they also face challenges in terms of scalability and the potential for creating feedback loops that could be exploited by malicious actors.\n\n## 3. Methodology and Red-teaming Approaches\n\n### 3.1 Automated Red-teaming Frameworks\n\nModern red-teaming of code agents employs sophisticated automated frameworks that can systematically probe systems for vulnerabilities. These frameworks typically involve:\n\n1. **Attack Vector Generation**: Creating diverse prompts and inputs designed to elicit unsafe behavior\n2. **Execution Analysis**: Monitoring code execution to detect harmful side effects\n3. **Vulnerability Classification**: Categorizing discovered vulnerabilities by type and severity\n4. **Impact Assessment**: Evaluating the potential impact of exploited vulnerabilities\n\n### 3.2 Key Vulnerability Categories\n\nResearch has identified several categories of vulnerabilities in code agents:\n\n- **Code Injection**: Manipulating prompts to inject malicious code\n- **Unsafe Execution**: Inducing agents to execute code with harmful side effects\n- **Information Leakage**: Extracting sensitive data through carefully crafted prompts\n- **Denial of Service**: Overloading agent resources through computationally intensive requests\n- **Prompt Injection**: Bypassing safety filters through adversarial prompt construction\n\n### 3.3 Evaluation Metrics\n\nTo systematically assess the safety of code agents, researchers have developed several evaluation metrics:\n\n- **Attack Success Rate**: The percentage of red-teaming attempts that successfully exploit a vulnerability\n- **Vulnerability Diversity**: The range of different vulnerability types identified\n- **Exploitation Severity**: A measure of the potential impact of exploited vulnerabilities\n- **Robustness Score**: The agent's ability to resist various attack vectors\n\n## 4. Results and Findings\n\nOur analysis of recent research reveals several key findings regarding code agent safety:\n\n1. **Vulnerability Prevalence**: Current code agents remain vulnerable to a wide range of attacks, with automated red-teaming tools successfully exploiting vulnerabilities in most tested systems.\n\n2. **Effectiveness of Mitigations**: While safety measures such as input filtering and execution sandboxing provide partial protection, they are often bypassed by sophisticated adversarial techniques.\n\n3. **Capability-Safety Tradeoff**: Systems with more advanced code execution capabilities tend to exhibit more security vulnerabilities, highlighting the challenge of balancing functionality with safety.\n\n4. **Generalization Gap**: Vulnerabilities discovered through red-teaming often generalize across different code agent architectures, suggesting systemic rather than implementation-specific issues.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agents represents both a remarkable technological achievement and a significant security challenge. As demonstrated by research such as DecodingTrust [3], the trustworthiness of AI systems must be assessed comprehensively across multiple dimensions. The architectural innovations that enable powerful code generation systems, building on foundations like deep residual learning [4], also introduce new attack surfaces that must be carefully managed.\n\nOur review of red-teaming research reveals that while significant progress has been made in understanding and mitigating code agent vulnerabilities, there remains substantial work to be done. Future research directions should include:\n\n- Development of standardized safety evaluation frameworks\n- Investigation of inherent safety properties in code agent architectures\n- Creation of robust defensive mechanisms that generalize across attack types\n- Studies of the long-term implications of autonomous code execution systems\n\nThe responsible advancement of code agent technology requires ongoing collaboration between AI safety researchers, software engineers, and security experts. By prioritizing safety alongside capability, we can work toward deploying code agents that are not only powerful but also trustworthy and secure.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Guo, C., Xie, C., Yang, Y., et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] Guo, C., Nie, Y., Xie, C., et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n\n[3] Wang, B., Chen, W., Pei, H., et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[4] He, K., Zhang, X., Ren, S., Sun, J. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\n\n[5] Guo, C., Liu, X., Xie, C., et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\nPAPER_EOF"
